en.prompt:
A colleague wanted a 4-stage shift register: output d should equal input in delayed by exactly 4 rising edges of clk. They wrote:

```verilog
module dly4 (input clk, input in, output reg d);
  reg a, b, c;
  always @(posedge clk) begin
    a = in;
    b = a;
    c <= b;
    d <= c;
  end
endmodule
```

Signals a, b and c are not used anywhere outside this block. There is no reset. In simulation all four regs (a, b, c, d) start at 0. Input in changes only on falling edges of clk, so it is stable at every rising edge. Its value at rising edges 1, 2, 3 and 4 is 1, 0, 1, 1.

(1) Give the values of a, b, c and d immediately after each of the four rising edges.
(2) By how many rising edges is d actually delayed relative to in, and why?
(3) How many flip-flops will synthesis infer? Will the synthesized hardware behave the same as this RTL simulation?
(4) Fix the code so that it does what was intended.

en.reference_solution:
(1) Within one edge, the blocking statements execute in order and update immediately. a = in, then b = a, so b also gets in. The non-blocking statements c <= b and d <= c are evaluated with the current values (b, already equal to in, and the old c) and updated at the end of the time step.

Edge 1 (in=1): a=1, b=1, c<=1, d<=old c=0. After: a=1 b=1 c=1 d=0
Edge 2 (in=0): a=0, b=0, c<=0, d<=1. After: a=0 b=0 c=0 d=1
Edge 3 (in=1): a=1, b=1, c<=1, d<=0. After: a=1 b=1 c=1 d=0
Edge 4 (in=1): a=1, b=1, c<=1, d<=1. After: a=1 b=1 c=1 d=1

(2) d is delayed by 2 edges, not 4. The value of in at edge 1 (1) appears on d after edge 2. The value at edge 2 (0) appears on d after edge 3. The value at edge 3 (1) appears on d after edge 4. The cause is that a and b are blocking assignments: b reads the value a received a moment earlier in the same edge, so in passes through a and b combinationally and is captured directly into c. Only c and d add a cycle each.

(3) Synthesis infers 2 flip-flops: c (D input = in) and d (D input = c). Every pass through the block writes a and b before reading them, and nothing outside reads them, so their value never has to survive from one edge to the next. They become wires. The hardware behaves exactly like this simulation, also showing a 2-edge delay. There is only one block, and the order inside it is fixed, so there is no race and no simulation/synthesis mismatch. The bug is in the design's function, not in the simulator.

(4) Make all four assignments non-blocking:

```verilog
module dly4 (input clk, input in, output reg d);
  reg a, b, c;
  always @(posedge clk) begin
    a <= in;
    b <= a;
    c <= b;
    d <= c;
  end
endmodule
```

Now every right-hand side reads the value from before the edge. a holds in delayed by 1 edge, b by 2, c by 3 and d by 4. Synthesis infers 4 flip-flops.

