- The clocked block becomes two D flip-flops (s1, s0) with asynchronous clear driven by rst_n. Their reset value is 00.
- The always @(*) block becomes pure combinational next-state logic from {s1, s0, req0, req1}. Because of the default assignment it infers no latch.
- The outputs are decoded from the state bits: gnt0 = s1·s0' and gnt1 = s1·s0. They are Moore outputs and depend only on the state.

(3) Trace, starting in IDLE_P0:
| Edge | (req0, req1) | Transition | State after edge | (gnt0, gnt1) |
|---|---|---|---|---|
| 1 | (1,0) | IDLE_P0 to G0 | G0 | 10 |
| 2 | (0,0) | G0 to IDLE_P1 (req0 dropped, no req1) | IDLE_P1 | 00 |
| 3 | (1,1) | IDLE_P1 to G1 (req1 has priority) | G1 | 01 |
| 4 | (0,1) | G1 holds | G1 | 01 |
| 5 | (0,0) | G1 to IDLE_P0 | IDLE_P0 | 00 |
| 6 | (1,1) | IDLE_P0 to G0 | G0 | 10 |

