en.reference_solution:
Bug 1, sensitivity list: the state register uses always @(posedge clk) without negedge rst_n. The reset is sampled only on a clock edge, so it is synchronous, while the spec requires it to be asynchronous. With no running clock the FSM never resets. Fix: always @(posedge clk or negedge rst_n).

Bug 2, reset polarity: if (rst_n) forces IDLE while rst_n = 1, which is normal operation. The FSM stays stuck in IDLE when reset is released and runs only while reset is asserted. Fix: if (!rst_n).

Bug 3, blocking assignments in the clocked block: state = ... updates state immediately in the active region. Another block triggered on the same posedge clk (here the gnt register) may read either the old or the new value, depending on the simulator's scheduling order. This is a race, and simulation may not match synthesis. Lint tools also flag blocking assignments in sequential logic. Fix: state <= ....

Bug 4, incomplete assignment in the next-state block: each case branch uses an if without an else, and there is no default branch. For example, in IDLE with req = 0, next is not assigned, and the encoding 2'd3 is not handled at all. next must therefore keep its previous value, so synthesis infers a level-sensitive latch on next instead of pure combinational logic. That causes a timing hazard and a lint warning, and simulation may disagree with the netlist. Fix: assign next = state; as the first statement of the block, so every path holds the current state by default, and add default: next = IDLE; so the illegal state 3 recovers.

Bug 5, registered output: gnt <= (state == GRANT) inside @(posedge clk) adds a flip-flop. gnt rises one cycle after the FSM enters GRANT and falls one cycle after it leaves. This violates the Moore spec. Example: the FSM enters GRANT at edge k, leaves at edge k+2. The spec gives gnt = 1 between edges k and k+2. The buggy code gives gnt = 1 between edges k+1 and k+3. The flip-flop also has no reset, so gnt is X until the first clock. Fix: decode state combinationally.

Corrected module:
```verilog
module req_ctrl (
  input  wire clk,
  input  wire rst_n,
  input  wire req,
  input  wire ack,
  output reg  gnt
);
  localparam IDLE = 2'd0, WAIT = 2'd1, GRANT = 2'd2;
  reg [1:0] state, next;

  // state register: async active-low reset, nonblocking
  always @(posedge clk or negedge rst_n) begin
    if (!rst_n)
      state <= IDLE;
    else
      state <= next;
  end

  // next-state logic: fully assigned, no latch
  always @(*) begin
    next = state;
    case (state)
      IDLE:    if (req)  next = WAIT;
      WAIT:    if (ack)  next = GRANT;
      GRANT:   if (!req) next = IDLE;
      default:           next = IDLE;
    endcase
  end

  // Moore output: pure decode of the current state
  always @(*)
    gnt = (state == GRANT);
endmodule
```
Synthesis result: two D flip-flops with asynchronous clear for state, and combinational gates for next and gnt. There are no latches and no extra flip-flop on gnt.

