en.reference_solution:
Defects:
(a) Line 9: clk is never initialized. It starts at X, and ~X = X, so clk never toggles. No posedge ever occurs, the initial block blocks forever at line 16, and the simulation never reaches $finish. It runs until it is killed and checks nothing. Fix: reg clk = 1'b0; or initial clk = 0; With this fix, posedges fall at 5, 15, 25 ns and negedges at 10, 20, 30 ns.
(b) Lines 16 and 22: din = 1 is a blocking assignment executed in the same time step as the posedge at which the DUT samples din. The order of the testbench process and the DUT always block is undefined, so the DUT may see the old value or the new one. The edge can land in either cycle depending on the simulator. Fix: change din only on the negedge (or use din <= 1, or add #1 after the edge).
(c) Lines 17-18: right after @(posedge clk) the if runs in the active region, before the DUT's nonblocking update of pulse for that same edge. It therefore reads the value from the previous edge. Combined with (b), the check passes or fails depending on event ordering: if the DUT caught din = 1 at 15 ns, the old value read at 25 ns is 1; if it caught it at 25 ns, the check reads 0. Fix: sample outputs on the negedge, half a cycle away from the update edge.
(d) Line 18: pulse != 1 evaluates to X when pulse is X, the if is not taken, and an X output passes silently. Fix: pulse !== 1'b1, or compare with !== against an expected value.
(e) Line 24: TEST PASSED is printed regardless of errors. Fix: if (errors == 0) PASS, else FAIL with the count.
Coverage gaps: only one rising edge is ever checked. Nothing checks that pulse returns to 0 after one cycle, that din held high gives only one pulse, that a falling edge gives no pulse, back-to-back 1-0-1 input, din already high when reset is released, or pulse during reset. Nothing after line 22 is checked at all.

Corrected testbench:
```verilog
`timescale 1ns/1ps
module tb;
  reg clk = 1'b0;
  reg rst_n, din;
  wire pulse;
  reg din_prev, exp_pulse;
  integer errors = 0, pulses = 0;

  edge_det dut (.clk(clk), .rst_n(rst_n), .din(din), .pulse(pulse));

  always #5 clk = ~clk;   // posedges 5,15,25,...  negedges 10,20,30,...

  // reference model written from the specification
  always @(posedge clk or negedge rst_n)
    if (!rst_n) begin din_prev <= 1'b0; exp_pulse <= 1'b0; end
    else begin exp_pulse <= din & ~din_prev; din_prev <= din; end

  // checker: every negedge, away from the update edge, X-safe
  always @(negedge clk) begin
    if (pulse !== exp_pulse) begin
      $display("ERROR t=%0t din=%b pulse=%b expected=%b", $time, din, pulse, exp_pulse);
      errors = errors + 1;
    end
    if (pulse === 1'b1) pulses = pulses + 1;
  end

  // stimulus: din changes only on negedge
  task drive(input v);
    begin @(negedge clk); din = v; end
  endtask

  initial begin
    rst_n = 1'b0;
    din   = 1'b1;             // din already high during reset
    #12 rst_n = 1'b1;         // released between edges; first posedge after release is 15 ns
    drive(0);                 // 20 ns
    drive(1); drive(1); drive(1);   // 30-50 ns: held high -> one pulse only
    drive(0); drive(0);       // 60-70 ns: falling edge -> no pulse
    drive(1); drive(0); drive(1);   // 80-100 ns: back-to-back -> two separate pulses
    drive(0); drive(0);       // 110-120 ns
    @(negedge clk); #1;       // 131 ns: last checker pass at 130 ns has completed
    if (pulses != 4) begin
      $display("ERROR: saw %0d pulses, expected 4", pulses);
      errors = errors + 1;
    end
    if (errors == 0) $display("TEST PASSED");
    else             $display("TEST FAILED: %0d errors", errors);
    $finish;
  end
endmodule
```
Expected trace (posedge: din sampled, previous sample, pulse seen at the next negedge):
- 15 ns: din 1, previous 0 (reset value), pulse = 1 at 20 ns. This is the case of din high at reset release.
- 25 ns: din 0, pulse = 0.
- 35 ns: din 1, previous 0, pulse = 1 at 40 ns.
- 45 ns and 55 ns: din 1, previous 1, pulse = 0. Held high, and the pulse width is one cycle.
- 65 ns and 75 ns: din 0, pulse = 0. A falling edge gives no pulse.
- 85 ns: din 1, pulse = 1 at 90 ns.
- 95 ns: din 0, pulse = 0.
- 105 ns: din 1, pulse = 1 at 110 ns.
- 115 ns and 125 ns: din 0, pulse = 0.
- Negedge at 10 ns (during reset): pulse = 0.

Total pulses = 4. The independent pulse count catches a bug shared by the model and the DUT. The per-cycle comparison catches wrong timing or wrong width.

