```verilog
module ctrl_blk (
  input  wire        clk,
  input  wire        por_n,
  input  wire        wr_en,
  input  wire [7:0]  addr,
  input  wire [7:0]  wdata,
  output reg  [15:0] count
);
  wire soft_rst  = wr_en & (addr == 8'h3C) & wdata[0];
  wire blk_rst_n = por_n & ~soft_rst;

  always @(posedge clk or negedge blk_rst_n)
    if (!blk_rst_n) count <= 16'd0;
    else            count <= count + 16'd1;
endmodule
```

Find the reset problems in this code and rewrite the module. The counter's reset must assert asynchronously and release synchronously to clk for both reset sources, and must never be triggered by bus glitches. Use a 2-flop reset synchronizer. Then trace your design for one soft-reset write: wr_en=1, addr=0x3C, wdata[0]=1 are sampled at rising edge E0, wr_en=0 at E1 and after, and por_n stays high. Say when count becomes 0 and at which edge it first increments.

en.reference_solution:
Problems:
(a) Glitches. soft_rst is a combinational AND of wr_en, an 8-bit compare and wdata[0]. After every clk edge these bits settle at different times. A transient combination can match 0x3C with wr_en=1 and wdata[0]=1 for a few hundred ps. blk_rst_n then dips and the asynchronous reset clears count immediately, even when no write to 0x3C happened. The clock does not filter an asynchronous reset.
(b) Soft-reset release is not synchronous. During a real write, soft_rst falls shortly after the next edge, after a delay set only by the decode path. The release can land inside the removal window of that edge, or the recovery window of the next one. Bits of count may then leave reset on different edges or go metastable.
(c) POR release is not synchronous. por_n deasserts at an arbitrary time relative to clk. This causes the same recovery/removal hazard, so count can start with a corrupted value.

Fixed RTL:
```verilog
module ctrl_blk (
  input  wire        clk,
  input  wire        por_n,
  input  wire        wr_en,
  input  wire [7:0]  addr,
  input  wire [7:0]  wdata,
  output reg  [15:0] count
);
  // POR synchronizer for the control flop
  reg [1:0] por_sync;
  always @(posedge clk or negedge por_n)
    if (!por_n) por_sync <= 2'b00;
    else        por_sync <= {por_sync[0], 1'b1};
  wire por_sync_n = por_sync[1];

  // Registered, glitch-free soft-reset request; reset by POR only
  reg soft_rst_q;
  always @(posedge clk or negedge por_sync_n)
    if (!por_sync_n) soft_rst_q <= 1'b0;
    else             soft_rst_q <= wr_en & (addr == 8'h3C) & wdata[0];

  // Block reset: asynchronous assert, synchronous release
  wire rst_req_n = por_n & ~soft_rst_q;
  reg [1:0] blk_sync;
  always @(posedge clk or negedge rst_req_n)
    if (!rst_req_n) blk_sync <= 2'b00;
    else            blk_sync <= {blk_sync[0], 1'b1};
  wire blk_rst_n = blk_sync[1];

  always @(posedge clk or negedge blk_rst_n)
    if (!blk_rst_n) count <= 16'd0;
    else            count <= count + 16'd1;
endmodule
```

Why each flop uses the reset it does:
- The decode is sampled only at clock edges, after it has settled, so soft_rst_q has no glitches.
- In operation por_n is a constant 1, so rst_req_n follows a single flop output and is clean. The path from soft_rst_q to the blk_sync clear pins is a normal same-clock path that STA times.
- soft_rst_q is reset by POR only. If blk_rst_n reset it, the request would clear itself asynchronously and rst_req_n would become a pulse as narrow as the loop delay.
- count's reset now always releases two edges after the request ends, synchronously to clk.

Timeline:
- E0: soft_rst_q goes 1 (clock-to-q after E0). rst_req_n goes 0, blk_sync is cleared asynchronously, blk_rst_n goes 0, and count becomes 0 shortly after E0.
- E1: soft_rst_q samples 0, so rst_req_n returns to 1 just after E1.
- E2: blk_sync becomes 01.
- E3: blk_sync becomes 11, so blk_rst_n rises just after E3. At E3 count is still held at 0.
- E4: count is first incremented, 0 to 1.

