en.prompt:
Two unrelated clocks: clk_a at 100 MHz and clk_b at 75 MHz. In the clk_a domain, a 3-bit register cfg_a[2:0] is driven by a flop. It is loaded with cfg_wdata_a[2:0] in any clk_a cycle where the one-cycle strobe cfg_wr_a is 1. Software may write any value, and writes are at least 1 µs apart. The reset rst_a_n is active low, and its assertion and deassertion are synchronous to clk_a. In the clk_b domain, a decoder uses cfg_b to pick one of 8 modes. Mode 3'b111 starts a flash erase. A colleague wrote this module and says it is CDC-safe because every bit has a two-flop synchronizer:

```verilog
module cfg_sync (
  input  wire       clk_b,
  input  wire       rst_a_n,
  input  wire [2:0] cfg_a,
  output reg  [2:0] cfg_b
);
  reg [2:0] s1, s2;
  always @(posedge clk_b or negedge rst_a_n) begin
    if (!rst_a_n) begin
      s1 <= 3'b000; s2 <= 3'b000; cfg_b <= 3'b000;
    end else begin
      s1    <= cfg_a;
      s2    <= s1;
      cfg_b <= s2;
    end
  end
endmodule
```

Review this design. You may change the clk_a-side logic as well, and you may add signals between the domains. For a software update from 3'b011 to 3'b100, which values can cfg_b show, and for how long? Find every CDC problem and write a corrected design in Verilog.

en.reference_solution:
**Problem 1: multi-bit bus synchronized bit by bit.**

The two-flop chains stop metastability from reaching cfg_b. They do not keep the three bits in step. When cfg_a changes close to a clk_b edge k, each bit of s1 independently captures either the old or the new value at edge k. A bit that captures the old value takes the new one at edge k+1, because cfg_a is then stable for at least 1 µs.

So for exactly one clk_b cycle (about 13.33 ns), cfg_b can hold any mix of old and new bits:
- bit 2 can be 0 or 1;
- bit 1 can be 1 or 0;
- bit 0 can be 1 or 0.

That covers all 8 combinations. Excluding the old value 011 and the new value 100, the transient values are 000, 001, 010, 101, 110 and 111.

Example: bit 2 captures the new value at edge k, and bits 1:0 capture it at edge k+1. Then cfg_b = 111 for one cycle, which starts a flash erase that software never requested.

Adding more synchronizer stages does not help. Gray code does not help either, because software can jump between arbitrary values.

**Problem 2: reset crossing.**

The clk_b flops are cleared by rst_a_n, whose deassertion is synchronous to clk_a and therefore asynchronous to clk_b. The release can violate recovery/removal. Some flops can leave reset one cycle later than others, or go metastable. Assertion may stay asynchronous. Deassertion must be synchronized to clk_b with a two-flop reset synchronizer, which produces rst_b_n.

**Fix: send one control bit, then capture the stable bus.**

```verilog
// clk_a domain
reg [2:0] cfg_a;
reg       tog_a;
always @(posedge clk_a or negedge rst_a_n)
  if (!rst_a_n) begin cfg_a <= 3'b000; tog_a <= 1'b0; end
  else if (cfg_wr_a) begin
    cfg_a <= cfg_wdata_a;
    tog_a <= ~tog_a;      // one transition per write
  end

// clk_b domain: reset synchronizer (async assert, sync deassert)
reg r1, r2;
always @(posedge clk_b or negedge rst_a_n)
  if (!rst_a_n) {r2, r1} <= 2'b00;
  else          {r2, r1} <= {r1, 1'b1};
wire rst_b_n = r2;

// clk_b domain: toggle synchronizer, edge detect, qualified capture
reg t1, t2, t3;
always @(posedge clk_b or negedge rst_b_n)
  if (!rst_b_n) begin
    t1 <= 1'b0; t2 <= 1'b0; t3 <= 1'b0; cfg_b <= 3'b000;
  end else begin
    t1 <= tog_a;          // only this flop can go metastable
    t2 <= t1;
    t3 <= t2;
    if (t2 ^ t3) cfg_b <= cfg_a;   // all 3 bits loaded in one edge
  end
```

**Why it works.**

Only the single bit tog_a is synchronized, and a single bit cannot be incoherent. cfg_a and tog_a change on the same clk_a edge. The capture enable t2 ^ t3 can be 1 no earlier than edge k+1 (when t1 caught the toggle at edge k), so the capture happens at edge k+2. By then cfg_a has been stable for more than one full clk_b period. The next write is at least 1 µs away, so cfg_a cannot move before the capture.

The cfg_a to cfg_b path should be constrained with a max delay of about one clk_b period, on the data path only.

Both tog_a and the t flops reset to 0, so leaving reset does not create a false edge. If software writes while B is still in reset, t1 picks up the new tog_a value after release and the value is still captured.

**Latency.**

| Delay component | clk_b periods |
|---|---|
| Wait for the first clk_b edge k | up to 1 |
| Extra edge if t1 resolves to the old value | 1 |
| t2, then capture at the edge after that | 2 |
| **Worst case total** | **4** |

4 × 13.33 ns ≈ 53.3 ns, which is far below the 1 µs write spacing.

